// spec: design.md, "Grammar parseable without name lookup", rule 3: in an expression `name<...>` is a template-id when what follows the `>` cannot continue a comparison (`(`, `::`, `;`, `)`, `,`, `==`, ...), and comparisons otherwise; a comparison of a comparison needs parentheses (design.md 22: `a < b > c` is an error, `(a < b) > c` compares twice), and `v<N> * 2` with a value as the argument would be such a chain, so `(v<N>) * 2` is written for the template-id
template<int N>
constexpr int scaled = N * 10;
template<typename T>
T twice(T x) { return x + x; }
int main()
{
  int a = 1, b = 2, c = 0;
  bool chain = (a < b) > c;                // parenthesised: compares twice
  int called = twice<int>(4);              // `(` follows: a template-id
  bool equal = scaled<3> == 30;            // `==` follows: a template-id
  int product = (scaled<2>) * 2;           // parenthesised: the template-id, then the product
  std::println(chain, " ", called, " ", equal, " ", product);
  return 0;
}
